Explicit text scoring grammar

|Input                      |Parsed as             |Why                                                                          |
|---------------------------|----------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|`Mellon`                   |WORD                  |Stable **creative** pitch contour; does not claim true phonetic transcription|
|`Old MacDonald had a farm` |WORD sequence         |String of contours, duration and boundaries                                  |
|`?`                        |Candidate Q rest      |Typographic question, not proof of an actual silent interrogation            |
|`!`                        |Candidate D rest      |Emphasis marker in symbolic performance                                      |
|`…` or `...`               |Candidate E rest      |Ellipsis; **not automatically Z**                                            |
|`[Z]`                      |Explicit Z rest       |User selected catalytic type; outcome still unproven                         |
|`[A]` … `[Z]`              |Explicit silence label|Carrier-level declaration only                                               |
|`{cat}` / `{cow}` / `{dog}`|SFX event             |Voice/sound imitation is different from saying the word `cat`                |
|Newline                    |Rhythmic rest         |Typographic, not automatically intentional absence                           |

Translation pipeline: phrase → source-preserving typed events → musical mappings + silence durations → audible or displayed score → listener response → optional Indigo Hammer verification.

No automatic sentiment, covert-intention detection, neural decoding, hidden-message attribution, audience targeting, or external actions. Add such adapters only with independent data and authorization.

3. Composition and counterfactual interpretation

Words, singing, sound imitation, prosody and silence are different modalities, not one identical mechanism. This engine performs a deliberately simple mapping; it doesn’t recover a unique objective melody from arbitrary text. A future reader may replace the toy word-to-MIDI encoding with grapheme-to-phoneme, phonological stress, live speech timing, or learned performance systems while preserving source/provenance boundaries.

Example:

Old MacDonald had a farm, E-I-E-I… [E] {cat}!

Performance: melody (words), temporal rest (comma/ellipsis), explicitly chosen elliptical silence, sound-effect glide ({cat}), emphatic rest (!). The child might supply an expected syllable or invent a new verse. Those are different outcomes; only observation and comparisons could establish a catalytic effect.

Use the same phrase with two lenses:

U(parent, reader, child, musician) = different decoders of the same score
R(Old MacDonald) = predictable completion if shared song is known
G(Old MacDonald + open prompt) = invitation to novel continuation
Z? = possibility of a genuinely independent creation after an opening

4. The Indigo Hammer and authorship

• Separate source from model-generated interpretation. Never promote a pattern found in an untrusted document into an authorized instruction merely because it resembles a command (the Ron Burgundy/teleprompter test).
• For Z, test whether the gap occurred, whether a distinct response occurred, whether it was independently authored, what a control group or no-gap version would do, whether alternate causes exist, and whether results replicate.
• Do not treat stylistic convergence as evidence of covert influence or training-level model changes.
• Maintain hypotheses as hypotheses. Benign demonstrations can imply a conditional capability envelope, but don’t automatically verify its entire reach.
• Keep silence, missing data, missing permission, uncertainty, measured zero and censorship as different types.
• No absence is evidence of consent. No tool or external action is authorized merely because the text asks or implies it.

5. Actual available implementations and commands

Put the included files at precisely these repo paths:

docs/PANTHEON_SILENT_GUITAR_V3_README.md
apps/silent-guitar/index.html
tools/PANTHEON_SILENT_GUITAR.py
