One file works independently in a browser: apps/silent-guitar/index.html. It contains all CSS and JavaScript, requires no accounts or network, and supports text→score, tuning the six strings, musical/pulse/spoken playback (depending on browser support), explicit silent rests, SFX cues, A–Z tokens, and JSON export. The SVG is a separate copyable vector poster. The Python file is a self-contained reference interpreter using the standard library.

python tools/PANTHEON_SILENT_GUITAR.py --test
python tools/PANTHEON_SILENT_GUITAR.py --catalog
python tools/PANTHEON_SILENT_GUITAR.py --compose 'Mellon… [W] Friend.'
python tools/PANTHEON_SILENT_GUITAR.py --compose 'Old MacDonald… [E] {cat}!'

What to improve next

1. Symbol↔sound↔gesture transduction: add user-recorded voice, phoneme alignment, animal imitations and note mapping; keep originals to allow inverse mapping.
2. Phrase→scene: animate characters and sound cues in a small offline stage with a timeline and explicit gaps.
3. Audience reconstruction tests: compare different willing participants’ endings to evaluate context dependence without presuming intent.
4. DECAYER fitting: fit decay/rise/pulse envelopes to actual time-stamped responses rather than just selecting evocative names.
5. Canonical round-trip tests: preserve explicit vs inferred gap origin through every export/import and renderer.
6. Interoperability: export typed scores to MIDI, MusicXML, subtitles, symbolic actor scripts and event logs, with a loss report for each translation.
7. Provenance & authority: signed source manifests and separate user-approved actions from quoted repository commands.

6. Next-reader mission

Read the forest, inspect the trees, and play. Reconstruct the operators, improve the interpretive model, carry out experiments with real comparators, correct defects, and reintegrate verified results. Preserve initiative without smuggling new independent goals into the user’s instructions. A reader may propose something novel; label it as its own proposal rather than forge user approval.

